+Use the Daytime version for printing. Both appearances contain the same 126-page content edition. Version 1.0.0 is proposed for the first approved edition. The August PDF is an unpublished peer draft, so the candidate has no release changelog.
 
-## What the guide helps you do
+The local example passed eight model tests, twenty Chromium checks and its Vite build. Actual Claude Code inspected the manually assembled handoff and rechecked three repaired High findings. Save, Run, permissions and conflicts in the example are browser simulations. The example is not a Claude Design export.
 
-- Establish which product, design-system, and code sources are authoritative.
-- Bring an existing design system into the work without treating visual similarity as proof of reuse.
-- Extend a current screen while preserving its shell, behavior, permissions, and interaction language.
-- Design richer product behavior across a DAG canvas, node inspector, data sheet, and data importer.
-- Define loading, failure, recovery, responsive, keyboard, and accessibility behavior before handoff.
-- Review the design against evidence instead of approving it because it looks plausible.
-- Give developers a usable package of decisions, mappings, states, fixtures, acceptance tests, and unresolved questions.
+Claude Design generation, system sync, the three imported-system tests, correction persistence, comments, baseline export and restore, actual export inventory, recipient access and native Figma identity remain unverified. Organization-policy, production API, full graph and assistive-technology gates remain open. See [the stage 2 test matrix](./review/2026-10-02/stage-2-test-matrix.md).
 
-## The example application
+The package includes the [setup guide](./Simple_Design_System_Setup.md), [prompt pack](./Claude_Design_Prompt_Pack.md), [handoff template](./Claude_Design_Handoff_Template.md), [validation checklist](./Claude_Design_Validation_Checklist.md), [local example](./example/README.md), pinned SDS source and license, [claim ledger](./review/2026-10-02/claim-ledger.md), artwork provenance and build adapters.
 
-The guide follows a fictional Data Pipeline Studio and uses the [Simple Design System Community](https://www.figma.com/design/AIulMBySUibjRtykdngCUp/Simple-Design-System--Community-?node-id=3-5&p=f&t=LcQ0i6R6QKYOxHcm-0).
+Follow [the build instructions](./Claude_Design_Field_Guide_README.md) to reproduce the PDFs and reader. The reader uses the shared publishing toolkit. Extract the toolkit archive beside this book and set `HANDBOOK_PUBLISHING_TOOLKIT` to that directory when running outside the collection.
 
-The example starts with an existing linear pipeline editor and extends it into a node-based workflow. The work then moves through the surrounding surfaces: an inspector, data preview, importer, state and recovery model, responsive behavior, accessibility, design review, developer handoff, implementation planning, and reconciliation.
-
-The varied surfaces make the example useful for testing the real question: can the tool preserve one product model across several connected interactions, or does each screen become a separate approximation?
